WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.069 --> 00:00:02.020
Let me give you an image.

00:00:02.020 --> 00:00:04.049
It's a cliche, but it's an image.

00:00:05.020 --> 00:00:07.020
- John Wayne.
- Oh God!

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:08.670
Couldn't we start with someone easier?

00:00:08.670 --> 00:00:11.909
Let me ask you a question: Has John Wayne ever been at a loss of words?

00:00:12.340 --> 00:00:18.930
Seriously. Go watch your favorite John Wayne classic and pay attention to how he speaks. Over his 40-year career, regardless of his age,

00:00:19.210 --> 00:00:22.380
character or mood, he almost always talks with the same iconic

00:00:22.620 --> 00:00:27.060
masculine confidence and control. He always has the right thing to say in as few words as possible.

00:00:27.480 --> 00:00:29.060
- I hope you die!

00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:31.940
- That'll be the day.

00:00:32.320 --> 00:00:35.020
Even when he's angry, he's not really angry. He never loses his cool.

00:00:35.220 --> 00:00:37.590
He never stammers, screams, or even improvises.

00:00:37.590 --> 00:00:39.640
- I haven't lost my temper in 40 years.

00:00:40.020 --> 00:00:44.280
But, Pilgrim, you caused a lot of trouble this morning mighta have got somebody killed, and

00:00:44.700 --> 00:00:47.660
somebody outta belt you in the mouth!

00:00:47.660 --> 00:00:51.800
Even at his worst, he doesn't lose that cool, John Wayne presence, and there's actually something amazing about that ability.

00:00:51.809 --> 00:00:58.180
And I'm not saying this makes him a bad actor. Actually, many studio Hollywood actors had a limited vocal range. Whether Humphrey Bogart was a

00:00:58.200 --> 00:00:59.380
private investigator:

00:00:59.380 --> 00:01:01.680
- He actually did shoot Thursby and Jacoby, didn't he?

00:01:01.700 --> 00:01:02.780
- a nightclub owner:

00:01:02.780 --> 00:01:05.980
- Where I'm going you can't follow. What I've got to do, you can't be any part of.

00:01:06.020 --> 00:01:07.260
- or even an Irishman:

00:01:07.260 --> 00:01:11.340
- [Irish accent] I was hoping that maybe in the state of mind you were in, he wouldn't ride tonight.

00:01:11.340 --> 00:01:13.500
He always had his trademark voice. It explains

00:01:13.500 --> 00:01:17.790
why some actors at that time are so easy to imitate. 
 
- You got any girlfriends with the same

00:01:17.920 --> 00:01:20.699
problem, bring 'em over. 
 
And John Wayne is no exception.

00:01:20.700 --> 00:01:24.340
- And so he went, [John Wayne imitation] "To be, or not to be?"

00:01:24.340 --> 00:01:25.800
[audience laughter]

00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:32.100
But John Wayne still seems to stand out. His complete control, his whole persona, has an almost mythic quality to it.

00:01:32.100 --> 00:01:34.980
- So you're a man's only good for one oath at a time?

00:01:34.980 --> 00:01:38.720
It's as if he's created a certain idea for masculine authority through his voice.

00:01:38.729 --> 00:01:41.188
There's an idea of talking like John Wayne.

00:01:41.250 --> 00:01:45.449
But post-classical method acting ushered in a wave of chameleon actors like Marlon Brando,

00:01:45.760 --> 00:01:49.180
Robert De Niro, and Meryl Streep- actors who morph to their voices and

00:01:49.260 --> 00:01:53.960
disappeared into their roles. Any attempt at imitating Daniel Day-Lewis would probably result in simply

00:01:54.060 --> 00:02:01.040
imitating one of his characters, not the actual actor, because he does them all so differently. The use of complex voice acting not only created

00:02:01.049 --> 00:02:06.569
more immersive performances, but actors and directors now use voice in more complicated ways to tell their story.

00:02:06.570 --> 00:02:08.639
It's not always about what a character says,

00:02:08.640 --> 00:02:14.009
but how and when they say it. Voice shows a character's control of a scene or lack of control.

00:02:14.009 --> 00:02:17.420
I want to use voice acting as a means for analyzing one of the most complicated

00:02:17.460 --> 00:02:20.240
characters in film: Daniel Plainview in There Will Be Blood.

00:02:20.240 --> 00:02:25.080
And I think to understand Daniel Plainview, you have to first understand talking like John Wayne.

00:02:25.080 --> 00:02:28.680
- [John Wayne imitation] Get off yer horse, and head into the saloon.

00:02:28.740 --> 00:02:34.860
John Wayne's big break came in 1939 in Stagecoach, and his introduction starts his streak of vocal control.

00:02:34.860 --> 00:02:35.440
- Hold it!

00:02:35.440 --> 00:02:40.920
Right from the beginning, John Wayne has a mastery of voice. After a long montage of stagecoach riding,

00:02:40.920 --> 00:02:46.530
he's the one that can stop the entire narrative with a single command. And notice what else is going on in his introduction shot:

00:02:46.530 --> 00:02:47.140
- Hold it!

00:02:47.140 --> 00:02:48.280
He immediately

00:02:48.280 --> 00:02:54.600
establishes himself as a master of his own movement through his flashy gun turn, the epic push-in of the camera toward his face and the

00:02:54.600 --> 00:02:57.959
mythical quality of the background, like he manifested out of the West.

00:02:58.230 --> 00:03:02.489
Here we see confidence fearlessness and mastery and the whole film stops to accommodate him.

00:03:02.489 --> 00:03:03.700
- Hey look, it's Ringo!

00:03:03.700 --> 00:03:07.620
The remainder of the scene presents another interesting phenomenon in voice.

00:03:07.660 --> 00:03:14.100
In 1939, editors were still tinkering with how to cut dialogue, and the camera would stay on a character most or all the time they were

00:03:14.100 --> 00:03:20.189
talking. Except for John Wayne. In this scene, you'll notice he's the only character that talks while the camera focuses on other characters.

00:03:20.189 --> 00:03:23.380
- Didn't expect to see you ridin' shotgun on this run, Marshall.

00:03:23.440 --> 00:03:28.040
As he talks, it cuts to reactions, or he talks away from the camera as he approaches the

00:03:28.150 --> 00:03:33.390
stagecoach. Even in his first big movie, even as Ringo the kid, and even right at his character

00:03:33.549 --> 00:03:39.239
introduction, his voice could control the movement all on its own. He has an almost directorial influence on the story.

00:03:39.239 --> 00:03:43.979
There's a distinct power to talking without being seen. In the same way that a narrator of a story has a

00:03:44.049 --> 00:03:50.189
God-like quality to them. If they talk while not in view, that means they're still controlling the scene even when they aren't there. In John

00:03:50.190 --> 00:03:55.679
Wayne's introductory scene, we see two methods of showing control: directing the narrative through voice and controlling the camera.

00:03:55.680 --> 00:03:57.900
And he's not the only one to control this way.

00:03:57.900 --> 00:04:01.140
- Why did you go to the police? Why didn't you go to me first?

00:04:01.140 --> 00:04:04.880
We actually also hear Daniel Plainview before we see him speaking.

00:04:04.880 --> 00:04:07.700
- I've traveled over half our state to be here tonight.

00:04:07.700 --> 00:04:12.120
Like John Wayne, he shows narrative control in his character introduction. He establishes

00:04:12.120 --> 00:04:16.700
he's the only one qualified to drill, and when people try to interrupt his narrative, he ignores them.

00:04:16.700 --> 00:04:18.260
- Well, what is your offer?

00:04:18.260 --> 00:04:20.980
- We're, we're wasting time, yes. Please!

00:04:20.980 --> 00:04:21.900
[frustrated chatter in background]

00:04:24.080 --> 00:04:25.580
- I do my own drilling.

00:04:25.580 --> 00:04:27.840
His authority does get challenged when the crowd uproars,

00:04:28.020 --> 00:04:32.740
but he simply leaves. Changing the narrative to what he wants, refusing to not have total control.

00:04:32.740 --> 00:04:35.280
- Too much confusion; thank you for your time.

00:04:35.280 --> 00:04:37.060
- No no no no, there's no confusion!

00:04:37.060 --> 00:04:40.080
- I wouldn't take the lease if you gave it to me as a gift.

00:04:40.120 --> 00:04:46.200
He even controls the movement of the camera: It stops when he stops. It moves when he moves. After another Voice of God

00:04:46.300 --> 00:04:49.300
transition, we see him get a lease how he wants, shifting the conversation

00:04:49.460 --> 00:04:55.660
to be about family to win them over. Another way to show control in a movie is through controlling the voices of others. This can

00:04:55.660 --> 00:04:57.730
be as simple as regulating when people talk:

00:04:57.730 --> 00:04:58.640
- Do you mean to insinuate-

00:04:58.640 --> 00:04:59.720
- Sit down, mister.

00:04:59.720 --> 00:05:01.840
Or regulating what they're allowed to say:

00:05:01.840 --> 00:05:03.160
[somber singing]

00:05:03.160 --> 00:05:04.660
- Put an "amen" to it!

00:05:04.660 --> 00:05:06.180
[singing continues]

00:05:06.180 --> 00:05:08.080
- There's no more time for prayer. Amen!

00:05:08.300 --> 00:05:14.420
We associate what people say with what they're thinking, what they believe in and what they want. If you control what someone can say, you

00:05:14.440 --> 00:05:16.300
control everything about them.

00:05:16.300 --> 00:05:17.820
- Say my name.

00:05:17.820 --> 00:05:20.300
In any scene, a character's voice can show control in these factors:

00:05:21.160 --> 00:05:27.020
how voice directs the narrative, how the voice controls the shot, and how characters control the voices of others. It's usually a

00:05:27.100 --> 00:05:28.300
combination of the three.

00:05:28.300 --> 00:05:31.860
- Say "what" again! I dare you! I double-dare you, motherfucker!

00:05:31.880 --> 00:05:37.080
In reality, everybody wishes they had the control of voice like John Wayne. Even when people disagree with him,

00:05:37.090 --> 00:05:42.160
he still drives the narrative and has complete mastery of the world around him. He may sometimes be a flawed hero,

00:05:42.160 --> 00:05:46.480
but the flaw is never in his voice, so he always has some level of control. In Red River

00:05:46.480 --> 00:05:47.560
he gets betrayed,

00:05:47.560 --> 00:05:50.380
but his voice makes it clear that he'll end up with what he wants.

00:05:50.420 --> 00:05:53.340
- Every time you turn around, expect to see me.

00:05:54.040 --> 00:05:56.360
- 'cause one time you'll turn around, and I'll be there.

00:05:56.360 --> 00:06:02.000
But that's the problem with John Wayne's authority: it never gets questioned. Even when he loses, he still talks in that

00:06:02.210 --> 00:06:05.950
confident air, never completely losing his mastery of the world of the West.

00:06:06.220 --> 00:06:10.239
What makes There Will Be Blood so well-written, is how carefully each scene tests power

00:06:10.340 --> 00:06:14.470
dynamics through voice. And Daniel Plainview's iconic presence does get challenged in

00:06:14.570 --> 00:06:20.799
inventive ways, to the point where the film seems to question whether or not a John Wayne-like vocal control is ever possible, or

00:06:21.009 --> 00:06:26.769
even desired. Daniel Plainview actually gets a pretty worthy opponent in the film. Eli Sunday is a gifted orator

00:06:26.770 --> 00:06:29.710
and he constantly questions Plainview's vocal authority.

00:06:29.710 --> 00:06:31.320
- Offer to use thirty-seven hundred dollars-

00:06:31.320 --> 00:06:32.960
- What is it that brought you here, sir?

00:06:32.960 --> 00:06:36.699
Take a seemingly benign conversation Plainview has with Eli Sunday:

00:06:36.699 --> 00:06:42.098
What appears to be Eli just making a simple request is actually a carefully coded fight for vocal control.

00:06:42.098 --> 00:06:44.098
- Daniel, may I speak with you?

00:06:44.098 --> 00:06:44.899
- Yes, come in.

00:06:44.900 --> 00:06:48.600
Eli starts with some innocent chitchat which Plainview doesn't engage in.

00:06:49.040 --> 00:06:50.060
- Spirits seem high.

00:06:52.520 --> 00:06:56.100
Then, Eli chooses to seat himself without being offered. Here

00:06:56.100 --> 00:07:00.680
we see Eli taking initiative, contrasting his brothers politeness in an earlier scene:

00:07:00.840 --> 00:07:01.380
- Can I sit down?

00:07:02.460 --> 00:07:03.060
- Please.

00:07:03.100 --> 00:07:07.440
The scene becomes about vocal control when Eli asks to speak at the induction of the well.

00:07:07.440 --> 00:07:08.800
- I will bless the well.

00:07:08.800 --> 00:07:13.160
Actually he doesn't ask. He says he will, directing the speech as he wants it.

00:07:13.160 --> 00:07:15.240
- Before you begin, you should introduce me.

00:07:15.240 --> 00:07:18.180
Of course, Plainview corrects him, but Eli ignores his correction.

00:07:18.300 --> 00:07:19.240
- We get it.

00:07:19.360 --> 00:07:20.940
- You'll see me walk up,

00:07:20.940 --> 00:07:22.680
He even provides him a script.

00:07:22.680 --> 00:07:24.020
- and then you could say:

00:07:24.260 --> 00:07:28.740
"The proud son of these hills, who tended his father's flock."

00:07:28.740 --> 00:07:30.740
Plainview baits him, letting him pick the time:

00:07:30.900 --> 00:07:32.260
- Well, let's make it four o'clock then.

00:07:32.580 --> 00:07:35.840
And then it cuts to the actual induction where Eli is supposed to speak,

00:07:35.860 --> 00:07:40.180
but Plainview directs it his way, pulling up the little girl and performing his own script.

00:07:40.180 --> 00:07:46.680
He does it all on his own, ignoring Eli's request. The camera hovers on Eli as Plainview blesses the well, and his voice of God

00:07:46.760 --> 00:07:50.960
dominates the scene and Eli. He even manipulates Eli's words and makes them his own.

00:07:51.060 --> 00:07:56.580
- Number one name for the merry Miss Sunday, here by my side. A proud daughter of these hills.

00:07:56.660 --> 00:08:01.300
In a simple sequence, Plainview gives up the win, lets Eli's guard down, and gains back control,

00:08:01.400 --> 00:08:07.839
directing the moment how he wanted: controlling the camera and controlling Eli's words. But Eli shouldn't be underestimated as a character.

00:08:07.839 --> 00:08:10.629
He speaks very well, and is the vocal leader of the town.

00:08:10.629 --> 00:08:13.479
It's two directors pitted against each other. One directs the

00:08:13.669 --> 00:08:20.198
congregation, the other directs the oil well. The next vocal showdown between the two comes in Eli's territory, when Plainview has to get baptized.

00:08:20.260 --> 00:08:24.840
Notice Eli already starts in control when we hear his voice before we see him speak.

00:08:25.160 --> 00:08:28.680
- I truly wish everyone could be saved, don't you?

00:08:29.000 --> 00:08:30.260
- [congregation responds] Yes.

00:08:30.580 --> 00:08:34.180
He directs when the congregation speaks. Of course, Plainview resists,

00:08:34.180 --> 00:08:38.020
but Eli subjugates him. He has to respond as directed by Eli.

00:08:38.180 --> 00:08:39.440
- What do you want me to say?

00:08:39.520 --> 00:08:43.300
Eli forces him to say exactly what he wants and what he's insecure about.

00:08:43.340 --> 00:08:45.860
- "I have abandoned my child!"

00:08:48.480 --> 00:08:50.440
- [hissing] Say it, say it..

00:08:50.520 --> 00:08:55.220
And in a complete reversal, Eli speaks as the voice of God throughout, while we focus on Plainview.

00:08:55.240 --> 00:08:57.240
It's a total opposite to the last confrontation:

00:08:57.580 --> 00:09:03.580
Eli gives him the lines and he has to recite them, forcing Plainview to, for the first time, lose his vocal control.

00:09:03.740 --> 00:09:05.860
- I've abandoned my child!

00:09:06.400 --> 00:09:08.200
- I've abandoned my boy!

00:09:08.200 --> 00:09:13.160
The next time they see each other is at the end of the movie. Even though Eli asks for help in this scene, he still

00:09:13.180 --> 00:09:19.120
starts off in control. The camera follows him and he asserts himself by making them drinks. When Plainview agrees to work with him,

00:09:19.120 --> 00:09:20.860
he says he'll do it on one condition.

00:09:20.860 --> 00:09:23.220
- I'd like you to tell me that you are

00:09:23.880 --> 00:09:26.540
- and have been a false prophet,

00:09:27.540 --> 00:09:29.680
- and that God is a superstition.

00:09:30.280 --> 00:09:32.240
And now Plainview is the director again.

00:09:32.240 --> 00:09:33.120
- That is a superstition-

00:09:33.240 --> 00:09:33.860
- Why don't you stand up?

00:09:35.360 --> 00:09:36.140
[throat clearing]

00:09:38.320 --> 00:09:39.280
- Put your glass down.

00:09:40.680 --> 00:09:45.860
In theory, Plainview has bested him, but it doesn't really feel like a John Wayne kind of vocal control.

00:09:45.860 --> 00:09:47.120
[unintelligible argument]

00:09:47.120 --> 00:09:47.900
- Don't say this to me-

00:09:47.900 --> 00:09:49.840
- I have broke you and I beat you.

00:09:49.840 --> 00:09:54.740
It's a bastardized version, because at the end Plainview wins, but he's not in control.

00:09:54.740 --> 00:09:58.000
[slurred yelling]

00:09:58.160 --> 00:10:03.440
He spirals completely out of control. Towards the end he foams from the mouth, stutters, screams and grunts.

00:10:03.440 --> 00:10:08.150
He loses mastery of everything in this scene, even his walk. When we think of John Wayne's grace,

00:10:08.150 --> 00:10:12.259
it's also in his walk. Plainview stumbles around in complete erratic chaos.

00:10:12.260 --> 00:10:15.020
- I am the Third Revelation!

00:10:15.180 --> 00:10:21.800
He doesn't have control at all. Another relationship that showcases his lack of vocal control is with his adopted son, H.W.

00:10:21.800 --> 00:10:26.240
At first they work together as partners, but when he becomes deaf they lose their ability to communicate.

00:10:26.240 --> 00:10:28.240
- I can't hear my voice.

00:10:28.360 --> 00:10:30.640
And in short, Plainview doesn't know what to do with him.

00:10:30.650 --> 00:10:35.090
He's no longer an asset to the company, and Plainview sends him away. Years later H.W.

00:10:35.090 --> 00:10:41.440
returns with his translator, and Plainview tries to control him vocally by forcing him to speak, becoming threatened when he considers

00:10:41.540 --> 00:10:43.080
H.W. a competitor.

00:10:43.580 --> 00:10:46.460
- You speak instead of your little dog. Woof woof woof woof woof woof woof!

00:10:46.700 --> 00:10:48.500
So again, he gets vocal control,

00:10:48.500 --> 00:10:49.470
but at what cost?

00:10:49.470 --> 00:10:55.220
It's cruel and doesn't feel like mastery. In a lot of what he says, Daniel Plainview may have the vocal influence,

00:10:55.220 --> 00:10:59.150
but he's a conman. In one of his monologues, he elegantly talks about the schools

00:10:59.150 --> 00:11:02.119
he'll build, the wheat he'll grow, and the importance of family values.

00:11:02.120 --> 00:11:04.460
But the visuals show only men and no schools,

00:11:04.560 --> 00:11:10.300
irrigation or farmland, just people preparing the drilling. His Voice of God Authority tricks us. Even though Plainview

00:11:10.320 --> 00:11:15.260
constantly talks about bringing wealth to the town, there are actually very few signs of him improving the town.

00:11:15.260 --> 00:11:20.659
There's a sort of false sense of control when he talks. He vocally controls Eli but not himself.

00:11:20.660 --> 00:11:25.700
He can't vocally control his son because he lost his hearing, but he tries anyway. And he can't control the work

00:11:25.700 --> 00:11:30.409
he does. He has to lie about the benefits of his drilling, and much of the dangers of work are out of his control.

00:11:30.510 --> 00:11:32.929
We expect Plainview's monologue to be true because

00:11:33.030 --> 00:11:39.769
narrators are supposed to guide the story. One John Wayne movie that follows these principles of narration is Red River, which has a monologue

00:11:39.769 --> 00:11:43.480
that's almost identical to Plainview's. If you replace oil drilling with cattle:

00:11:43.480 --> 00:11:45.480
- Good beef for hungry people,

00:11:46.180 --> 00:11:48.180
beef to make 'em strong,

00:11:48.580 --> 00:11:49.740
make 'em grow.

00:11:49.740 --> 00:11:51.800
The key difference though is that John Wayne's hyper-masculine

00:11:51.880 --> 00:11:54.040
advertisement of his cattle business is actually

00:11:54.040 --> 00:11:59.620
taken seriously, whereas There Will Be Blood codes Plainview as a fraud. John Wayne's movies seem to have  some

00:11:59.640 --> 00:12:04.920
unrealistic ideals for heroes, whereas There Will Be Blood shows the more sinister sides of being in control.

00:12:04.920 --> 00:12:09.630
And I know I said John Wayne always has vocal control, but even he loses it in some of his later movies:

00:12:09.630 --> 00:12:13.000
[unintelligible yelling]

00:12:13.000 --> 00:12:14.180
[gunfire]

00:12:14.180 --> 00:12:18.460
His chaotic shooting of buffalo sheds light on the darker side to his supposed mastery.

00:12:18.670 --> 00:12:24.899
Regardless. many of his earlier films create an idea of a hyper-masculine male with complete control of what they say and how they say

00:12:24.899 --> 00:12:27.449
it. There's a certain fantasy to sounding like John Wayne.

00:12:27.449 --> 00:12:29.700
- If you can't learn the easy way, you learn the hard way.

00:12:29.760 --> 00:12:33.620
But movies like There Will Be Blood bring into question the validity of such a

00:12:33.680 --> 00:12:41.120
one-dimensional kind of control. Having such vocal authority actually doesn't end well. If someone really had as manly a dominion over their world,

00:12:41.130 --> 00:12:44.519
they wouldn't end up like a cool suave John Wayne riding into the sunset.

00:12:44.620 --> 00:12:47.459
They'd probably become the erratic, miserable and lonely

00:12:47.560 --> 00:12:53.729
Daniel Plainview, because at the end of the day, the old western heroes aren't real. That level of mastery is not only unrealistic,

00:12:53.730 --> 00:12:55.649
but also dangerous. Think about it:

00:12:55.649 --> 00:12:58.529
Why couldn't Daniel Plainview have let Eli Sunday give that speech?

00:12:58.529 --> 00:13:02.900
What difference does it make if he gave his little blessing? Daniel Plainview explains it himself:

00:13:02.900 --> 00:13:04.900
- I have a competition in me;

00:13:06.460 --> 00:13:08.220
I want no one else to succeed.

00:13:08.260 --> 00:13:13.840
And maybe that hyper-masculine unfaltering thinking is no way to live, and no fantasy to strive towards.

00:13:13.840 --> 00:13:16.360
- Never apologize, mister. It's a sign of weakness.

00:13:16.420 --> 00:13:22.740
When studying voice, John Wayne makes for a great place to start. His way of speaking is hyperbolic and he represents the ability to

00:13:22.840 --> 00:13:29.020
vocally master a scene. His purist vocal power, however, feels unrealistic and dangerous. When studying more vocally

00:13:29.040 --> 00:13:33.560
complicated characters, one can use John Wayne's power as a starting point and see how a character

00:13:33.699 --> 00:13:36.119
succeeds in the John Wayne level of vocal control,

00:13:36.120 --> 00:13:42.149
and how they fail. Do they control the camera, do they control the narrative and do they control others, and also at what cost?

00:13:42.640 --> 00:13:45.659
